\title{Introduction to the proof: from subspaces to crossings}
\taxon{Overview}
\meta{dependencies}{None.}
\meta{provenance}{[OpenAI, 23 September 2026](cn-0042), Section 1, from the paragraph containing equation (1.2) to the end of the introduction; rewritten with explicit notation and links on 9 October 2026.}

\p{The first ingredient is an inequality about subspaces. Fix an integer #{m\geq1}, a real vector space #{W} of finite dimension #{N}, and two indexed families of linear subspaces #{U_1,\ldots,U_m} and #{V_1,\ldots,V_m} of #{W}. Suppose #{U_i\cap V_i=\{0\}} for every #{i}; that is, each [designated pair](cn-0076) #{(U_i,V_i)} is disjoint. The [subspace-intersection inequality](cn-0024) states that}

##{\sum_{i,j=1}^{m}\dim(U_i\cap V_j)\leq\left\lfloor\frac{m^2}{4}\right\rfloor N.\startverb\tag{1.2}\stopverb}

\p{The sum runs over all #{m^2} ordered pairs of indices. Its diagonal terms, with #{i=j}, are zero by hypothesis; intersections with #{i\ne j} can have positive dimension. Thus (1.2) bounds the total of these intersection dimensions in terms of the number of pairs of subspaces and the dimension of their common ambient space.}

\p{To prove this inequality, choose distinct real [interpolation nodes](cn-0063) #{t_1,\ldots,t_m} and consider [univariate polynomial maps](cn-0064) #{P:\mathbb R\to W} subject to #{P(t_i)\in U_i}. The [constrained evaluation spaces](cn-0019) record how their values can vary as the degree increases. [Determinant multiplicity](cn-0021) turns the restrictions at the nodes into a bound on polynomial degrees. The [algebraic branch](cn-0061) gives the complete argument leading to (1.2).}

\p{The useful consequence for crossings is a bound on matrix ranks. Fix a positive integer #{d}, and let #{X_1,\ldots,X_m} and #{Y_1,\ldots,Y_m} be real #{d\times d} matrices. Here #{\operatorname{rank}} is the dimension of the image of the corresponding linear map on #{\mathbb R^d}. Assume every diagonal difference #{X_i-Y_i} is invertible. Apply (1.2) in the ambient space #{W=\mathbb R^d\oplus\mathbb R^d}, of dimension #{N=2d}, to the [graph subspaces](cn-0026)}

##{\begin{aligned}
U_i&=\Gamma_{X_i}=\{(v,X_iv):v\in\mathbb R^d\},\\
V_j&=\Gamma_{Y_j}=\{(v,Y_jv):v\in\mathbb R^d\}.
\end{aligned}}

\p{A vector belongs to both graphs exactly when its first coordinate #{v} satisfies #{(X_i-Y_j)v=0}. Hence}

##{\dim(U_i\cap V_j)=d-\operatorname{rank}(X_i-Y_j).}

\p{In particular, invertibility of #{X_i-Y_i} gives the required [diagonal disjointness](cn-0076). Recall the [balanced pair count](cn-0006) #{d_r=\lfloor r/2\rfloor\lfloor(r-1)/2\rfloor} for a positive integer #{r}. Substituting the displayed dimension formula into (1.2) and removing the #{m} diagonal rank terms gives the [rank-sum corollary](cn-0027):}

##{\sum_{i\ne j}\operatorname{rank}(X_i-Y_j)\geq2d_m d.}

\p{This sum is again over ordered pairs. The integer #{d} is the size of the matrices; the subscripted integer #{d_m} is the balanced pair count determined by #{m}. We will construct matrices whose off-diagonal ranks are bounded by crossings and whose diagonal differences are invertible.}

\p{For the main part of the drawing argument, assume #{m\geq3} and #{n=2s+1\geq3}, where #{s} is a positive integer. Set #{d=s^2}; then #{d=d_n}. The second algebraic ingredient is the linear [rank detector](cn-0032)}

##{\Phi:\mathbb R^{n\times n}\longrightarrow\mathbb R^{d\times d}.}

\p{Its input and output sizes serve different purposes: an input matrix will compare the #{n} edges in one vertex star with the #{n} edges in another, while the output will enter the rank-sum inequality above. Three properties of #{\Phi} make this possible.}

\p{First, let #{\mathcal L\subseteq\mathbb R^{n\times n}} be the space of [row-plus-column matrices](cn-0017): matrices #{H} whose entries have the form #{H(p,q)=r_p+c_q} for some vectors #{r,c\in\mathbb R^n}. The detector [annihilates](cn-0033) both #{\mathcal L} and every diagonal input matrix. Adding either kind of matrix to #{H} therefore leaves #{\Phi(H)} unchanged.}

\p{Second, the contribution of a single input entry to #{\Phi(H)} has rank at most one, and a diagonal input entry contributes zero. Consequently the [support rank bound](cn-0033) gives, for every #{H\in\mathbb R^{n\times n}},}

##{\operatorname{rank}\Phi(H)\leq\#\{(p,q):1\leq p,q\leq n,\ p\ne q,\ H(p,q)\ne0\}.}

\p{The right-hand side counts the nonzero off-diagonal entries of #{H}. This is how a bound on matrix entries will become a bound on crossings.}

\p{Third, for a linear order #{\prec} on #{\{1,\ldots,n\}}, let #{S_\prec} be its [half-order matrix](cn-0011): its entry is #{+1/2} when #{p\prec q}, #{-1/2} when #{q\prec p}, and #{0} when #{p=q}. The [order-invertibility lemma](cn-0034) proves}

##{\operatorname{rank}\Phi(S_\prec)=d\qquad\text{for every linear order }\prec.}

\p{The same map #{\Phi} works for all these orders. The [detector branch](cn-0062) constructs it from [barycentric weights](cn-0030) and [bivariate evaluation vectors](cn-0028), using one fixed tuple of distinct real nodes. [Two-triangle interpolation](cn-0029) supplies the invertibility argument.}

\p{We now obtain the input matrices from a drawing. Write the vertex classes of [#{K_{m,n}}](cn-0002) as #{\{a_1,\ldots,a_m\}} and #{\{b_1,\ldots,b_n\}}. Begin with any [admissible drawing](cn-0004) #{D_0}. By [normalization](cn-0009), replace it by a [normalized drawing](cn-0008) #{D}, with polygonal edges and no adjacent-edge crossings, such that its [crossing count](cn-0005) satisfies #{c(D)\leq c(D_0)}. A lower bound for #{c(D)} will therefore give the same lower bound for #{c(D_0)}.}

\p{Orient the edge #{a_i b_p} from #{a_i} to #{b_p}, and abbreviate it to #{ip}. We use #{i,k\in\{1,\ldots,m\}} for [first-class](cn-0002) vertices and #{p,q\in\{1,\ldots,n\}} for [second-class](cn-0002) vertices. The [star at #{a_i}](cn-0002) consists of its #{n} incident edges #{i1,\ldots,in}.}

\p{At each vertex #{v}, choose a gap between [outward spokes](cn-0075) and read them counterclockwise from that gap. The resulting [spoke comparison sign](cn-0011) #{T_v(e,f)} is #{+1} if the spoke of #{e} precedes that of #{f}, #{-1} for the reverse order, and #{0} if #{e=f}. For two distinct oriented edges #{e,f}, let #{I(e,f)} be their [signed interior intersection count](cn-0010), summing the signs of all their crossing points.}

\p{The [endpoint-corrected intersection matrix](cn-0013) #{J} is a real #{mn\times mn} matrix whose rows and columns are indexed by edges. Set #{J(e,e)=0}. If #{e,f} are independent, set #{J(e,f)=I(e,f)}; if they share a vertex #{v}, add the endpoint term #{\tfrac12T_v(e,f)}. This form of the endpoint term uses our orientation: at any shared vertex, both incident edges point outward or both point inward.}

\p{Group the edges by their first-class endpoints. The resulting #{n\times n} [star block](cn-0013) #{J^{ik}} compares the star at #{a_i} with the star at #{a_k}:}

##{J^{ik}(p,q)=J(ip,kq).}

\p{The endpoint corrections make the [cycle-pairing identity](cn-0016) hold. Its topological input is that the [mutual signed intersection](cn-0069) of two closed polygonal walks meeting transversely away from their corners in the plane is [zero](cn-0015). Applying the cycle identity to suitable four-cycles gives the [additive block relation](cn-0018)}

##{J^{ik}-J^{i1}-J^{1k}+J^{11}\in\mathcal L.}

\p{Here #{a_1} is a fixed reference vertex, and #{\mathcal L} is the row-plus-column space defined above. Apply #{\Phi} to each block and write #{R^{ik}=\Phi(J^{ik})}, a #{d\times d} matrix. Since #{\Phi} annihilates #{\mathcal L}, the relation becomes #{R^{ik}=R^{i1}+R^{1k}-R^{11}}. Thus setting}

##{X_i=R^{i1},\qquad Y_k=R^{11}-R^{1k}}

\p{gives precisely the required difference form #{R^{ik}=X_i-Y_k}. These are the matrices to which we apply the rank-sum corollary.}

\p{On the diagonal, all edges in the star at #{a_i} share that vertex, so normalization has removed their interior crossings. Therefore #{J^{ii}} is exactly the [half-order matrix](cn-0011) of the spoke order at #{a_i}. The detector's invertibility property gives #{\operatorname{rank}R^{ii}=d}; equivalently, every #{X_i-Y_i} is invertible. This concerns diagonal star blocks, each an #{n\times n} matrix, rather than diagonal entries within an input matrix.}

\p{For #{i\ne k}, let #{c_{ik}} be the number of crossing points between an edge in the star at #{a_i} and an edge in the star at #{a_k}. In #{J^{ik}}, entries with #{p=q} can have an endpoint correction at #{b_p}, but the detector annihilates these diagonal input entries. For #{p\ne q}, the two edges #{ip,kq} are independent, so #{J^{ik}(p,q)=I(ip,kq)}. Each nonzero such entry requires at least one crossing of that pair. The support rank bound therefore yields}

##{\operatorname{rank}R^{ik}\leq c_{ik}\qquad(i\ne k).}

\p{The count #{c_{ik}} includes every crossing point, even when the same two edges cross repeatedly. Cancellation between positive and negative crossings can make a signed entry smaller or zero; it does not invalidate this rank bound.}

\p{The [odd-size lower bound](cn-0035) now follows by putting the two rank estimates together, using #{d=d_n}:}

##{2d_m d_n\leq\sum_{i\ne k}\operatorname{rank}R^{ik}\leq\sum_{i\ne k}c_{ik}=2c(D).}

\p{Both sums run over ordered pairs of distinct first-class indices. Every crossing of the normalized drawing involves two distinct first-class stars and is counted once in each order, which explains the factor #{2} on the right. Dividing by #{2} gives #{c(D)\geq d_m d_n}, and hence #{c(D_0)\geq d_m d_n}.}

\p{For even #{n>2}, the [vertex-deletion argument](cn-0037) transfers the lower bound from odd second-class sizes. The [balanced-axis construction](cn-0036) gives a drawing with exactly #{d_m d_n} crossings, supplying the matching upper bound. If either class has at most two vertices, this upper bound is zero. Together these steps prove [the crossing-number formula](cn-0007).}
